WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.000
Part 4. You will hear a zoology student giving a presentation about an animal called a tardigrade.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:14.000
First, you have some time to look at questions 31 to 40.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:50.000
You will hear a student giving a presentation about an animal called a tardigrade.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:01:12.000
Now listen carefully and answer questions 31 to 40.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:16.000
For my project on invertebrates, I chose to study tardigrades.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:21.000
These are microscopic, or to be more precise, near-microscopic animals.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:29.000
There are well over a thousand known species of these tiny animals which belong to the phylum Tardigrada.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:41.000
Most tardigrades range in length from 0.05 to 1 mm, though the largest species can grow to be 1.2 mm in length.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:50.000
They are also sometimes called water bears, water because that's where they thrive best, and bear because of the way they move.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:57.000
Moss piglet is another name for tardigrades because of the way they look when viewed from the front.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:07.000
They were first discovered in Germany in 1773 by Johann Goetze, who coined the name Tardigrada.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:12.000
As I say, there are many different species of tardigrade.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:19.000
Too many to describe here, but generally speaking, the different species share similar physical traits.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:29.000
They have a body which is short and also rounded, a bit like a barrel, and the body comprises four segments.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:36.000
Each segment has a pair of legs, at the end of which are between four and eight sharp claws.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:40.000
I should also say that some species don't have any claws.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:46.000
What they have are discs, and these work by means of suction.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:52.000
They enable the tardigrade to cling on to surfaces or to grip its prey.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:57.000
Within the body, there are no lungs or any organs for breathing at all.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:05.000
Instead, oxygen and also blood are transported in a fluid that fills the cavity of the body.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:14.000
As far as the tardigrade's head is concerned, the best way I can describe this is that it looks rather strange, a bit squashed even,

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:22.000
though many of the websites I looked at described its appearance as cute, which isn't exactly very scientific.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:31.000
The tardigrade's mouth is a kind of tube that can open outwards to reveal teeth-like structures known as stylets.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:36.000
These are sharp enough to pierce plant or animal cells.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:39.000
So where are tardigrades found?

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:47.000
Well, they live in every part of the world, in a variety of habitats, most commonly on the bed of a lake,

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:52.000
or on many kinds of plants, or in very wet environments.

00:03:52.000 --> 00:04:01.000
There's been some interesting research which has found that tardigrades are capable of surviving radiation and very high pressure,

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:08.000
and they're also able to withstand temperatures as cold as minus 200 degrees centigrade,

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:15.000
or highs of more than 148 degrees centigrade, which is incredibly hot.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:26.000
It has been said that tardigrades could survive long after human beings have been wiped out,

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:29.000
even in the event of an asteroid hitting the Earth.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:38.000
If conditions become too extreme and tardigrades are at risk of drying out, they enter a state called cryptobiosis.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:46.000
They curl into a ball called a tun, that's T-U-N, by retracting their head and legs,

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:51.000
and their metabolism drops to less than 1% of normal levels.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:59.000
They can remain like this until they're reintroduced to water, when they will come back to life in a matter of a few hours.

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:07.000
While in a state of cryptobiosis, tardigrades produce a protein that protects their DNA.

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:15.000
In 2016, scientists revived two tardigrades that had been tuns for more than 30 years.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:22.000
There was a report that, in 1948, a 120-year-old tun was revived.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:25.000
But this experiment has never been repeated.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:34.000
There are currently several tests taking place in space to determine how long tardigrades might be able to survive there.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:38.000
I believe the record so far is 10 days.

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:40.000
So, moving on.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:47.000
In terms of their diet, tardigrades consume liquids in order to survive.

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:51.000
Although they have teeth, they don't use these for chewing.

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:57.000
They suck the juices from moss or extract fluid from seaweed.

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:03.000
But some species prey on other tardigrades, from other species or within their own.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:12.000
I suppose this isn't surprising, given that tardigrades are mainly comprised of liquid and are coated with a type of gel.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:17.000
Finally, I'd like to mention the conservation status of tardigrades.

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:28.000
It is estimated that they have been in existence for approximately half a billion years and, in that time, they have survived five mass extinctions.

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:41.000
So it will probably come as no surprise to you that tardigrades have not been evaluated by the International Union for Conservation of Nature and are not on any endangered list.

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:46.000
Some researchers have described them as thriving.

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:50.000
Does anyone have any questions they'd like to ask?

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:55.000
That is the end of Part 4.

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:59.000
You now have one minute to check your answers to Part 4.

